    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Enzymes are the best catalysts we know of: they not only perform all the chemical reactions required for life, but they enhance the rates of these reactions by orders of magnitude. Most of what is known about enzyme function has focused on the roles played by a small subset of active site residues. These have been shown to form “functional networks,” or groups of residues that enhance reaction rates in a highly cooperative manner. Residues outside of the active site (within the enzyme scaffold) are almost certainly connected to these functional groups. To date, however, limitations in current technologies have prevented us from mapping functional connections between scaffold residues and the active site. To address this challenge, the Fordyce lab has reengineered second-generation MITOMI (Mechanically Induced Trapping of Molecular Interactions) microfluidic devices to create a unique microfluidics assay capable of quantitatively measuring reaction rates for thousands of enzyme mutants simultaneously. Here, we explored several gene and protein synthesis techniques to make the process of preparing enzymatic DNA for the microfluidic assay both faster and cheaper. The most promising strategy explored was coupling a new yeast-based library generation and parsing technique, Recombinase Directed Indexing (REDI), followed by colony yeast PCR. These technologies will be used to conduct parallelized mutant cycles throughout the entire structure, thereby quantitatively mapping (for the first time for any enzyme) functional networks that connect distal residues to the active site to affect catalysis. This, in turn, will give us a more complete understanding of how enzymes achieve proficient catalysis, and suggest new routes to the design of novel enzymes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
